The classic interview use is finding the first repeated value in one pass. Read the return value of `add()` and stop as soon as it is `false`.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DuplicateDetection {
    // Find the first duplicate in O(n) time, O(n) space
    static Long findFirstDuplicate(List<Long> orderIds) {
        var seen = new HashSet<Long>();
        for (var id : orderIds) {
            if (!seen.add(id)) {   // add returned false -> duplicate
                return id;
            }
        }
        return null;               // no duplicates
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var orders = List.of(101L, 102L, 103L, 102L, 104L);
        System.out.println("First duplicate: " + findFirstDuplicate(orders)); // 102

        // Process each id exactly once -> act only when add returns true
        var processed = new HashSet<Long>();
        for (var id : orders) {
            if (processed.add(id)) {
                System.out.println("Processing: " + id);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The `!seen.add(id)` line is the trick: one call inserts the id and tells you whether it was already there, with no extra `contains()` lookup.

The goal here is just collecting unique ids. Order never gets read, yet every operation pays the tree tax.

```java
// Goal: just collect unique customer IDs — order never matters
Set<Long> seen = new TreeSet<>();   // O(log n) per add, for no reason
for (Long id : orderIds) {
    seen.add(id);                   // every insert walks the tree
}
boolean isNew = seen.contains(99L); // O(log n) membership test
// Sorted output is never read — the ordering cost is pure waste.
```

Match the structure to the requirement: `HashSet` for fast uniqueness, `TreeSet` only when order or ranges actually get used.

```java
// Membership / dedupe only -> HashSet (O(1))
Set<Long> seen = new HashSet<>();
for (Long id : orderIds) seen.add(id);
boolean isNew = seen.contains(99L);     // O(1)

// Need sorted iteration or ranges -> TreeSet (O(log n))
TreeSet<Long> sorted = new TreeSet<>(seen);
System.out.println(sorted.first());     // smallest ID
System.out.println(sorted.last());      // largest ID
NavigableSet<Long> window =
        sorted.subSet(1000L, true, 2000L, true); // IDs in [1000,2000]
```

This snippet shows the behaviour that falls out of the `HashMap` backing: duplicate rejection, one null, and the `equals`/`hashCode` contract for custom objects.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class OrderIdSet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var orderIds = new HashSet<Long>();

        // Behind the scenes: map.put(101L, PRESENT)
        System.out.println(orderIds.add(101L)); // true  (new key)
        System.out.println(orderIds.add(101L)); // false (key already there)

        orderIds.add(null);                     // allowed: one null key
        System.out.println(orderIds.contains(null)); // true

        // Custom object needs correct hashCode/equals
        record Order(Long id, String item) {}   // record auto-generates both
        var orders = new HashSet<Order>();
        orders.add(new Order(101L, "Laptop"));
        System.out.println(orders.contains(new Order(101L, "Laptop"))); // true
    }
}
```

The `record` gets correct `equals`/`hashCode` for free, so a freshly built equal `Order` lands in the same bucket and `contains()` finds it.

The first set uses a safe multi-field comparator. The second shows the duplicate trap when the comparator looks at only one field.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CustomSortedOrders {
    record Order(Long id, String item, int priority) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Sort by priority (high to low), then by id as a tiebreaker
        var byPriority = new TreeSet<Order>(
            Comparator.comparingInt(Order::priority).reversed()
                      .thenComparingLong(Order::id));
        byPriority.add(new Order(101L, "Laptop", 1));
        byPriority.add(new Order(102L, "Mouse", 3));
        byPriority.add(new Order(103L, "Keyboard", 3)); // kept: id breaks the tie
        System.out.println("Safe size: " + byPriority.size()); // 3

        // DANGER: comparator on priority alone treats equals as duplicates
        var byPriorityOnly = new TreeSet<Order>(Comparator.comparingInt(Order::priority));
        byPriorityOnly.add(new Order(101L, "Laptop", 1));
        byPriorityOnly.add(new Order(102L, "Mouse", 1)); // dropped: same priority
        System.out.println("Trap size: " + byPriorityOnly.size()); // 1, not 2
    }
}
```

The `thenComparingLong(Order::id)` tiebreaker is what saves the first set from collapsing two same-priority orders into one.
